<body>
    <div class="hero">
        <h1>🎥 Overlay Your Agenda on Video with OBS</h1>
        <p>Put your AgendaClock timeline right on top of your webcam feed, so everyone in your Zoom, Meet, or Teams call sees the meeting progress live — no screen sharing needed.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="wrap">
        <div class="step">
            <h2>What you're building</h2>
            <p>OBS Studio composites your webcam and your meeting timeline into one video feed, then exposes it as a <strong>virtual camera</strong>. In your meeting app you simply pick "OBS Virtual Camera" instead of your webcam. Your agenda stays on screen for the entire call, quietly keeping everyone on time.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><a href="https://obsproject.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Download OBS Studio</a> (free, Windows/macOS/Linux)</li>
                <li>Your meeting must be marked <strong>Public link</strong> in the editor — OBS loads the page without a login.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="step">
            <span class="badge-path">Recommended · No chroma key needed</span>
            <h2>Path 1: Browser Source with a transparent background</h2>
            <ol>
                <li>In OBS, create a Scene and add a <strong>Video Capture Device</strong> source — that's your webcam.</li>
                <li>Add a <strong>Browser</strong> source <em>above</em> the webcam in the Sources list.</li>
                <li>In the editor's Share panel, click <strong>🎥 Copy OBS URL</strong> and paste it as the URL. It looks like:<br>
                    <code>https://agendaclock.com/meeting/&lt;your-id&gt;?view=overlay&amp;chrome=0&amp;transparent=1</code></li>
                <li>Set <strong>Width 1920, Height 1080</strong> (or your canvas size).</li>
                <li>Done — <code>transparent=1</code> renders the page with a real alpha channel, so your camera shows through automatically. No filters required.</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="tip">💡 The <code>chrome=0</code> part hides on-screen hints and the view switcher so only the timeline is drawn.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="step">
            <span class="badge-path">Alternative · Window capture</span>
            <h2>Path 2: Window Capture + Chroma Key</h2>
            <p>If you'd rather show a normal browser window (or you're on a setup where the Browser source is unavailable):</p>
            <ol>
                <li>Open your timeline in a browser window: <code>…/meeting/&lt;your-id&gt;?view=overlay&amp;chrome=0</code>. The background is a solid blue <strong>#0047BB</strong> — that's intentional.</li>
                <li>In OBS add a <strong>Window Capture</strong> source above your webcam and pick that browser window.</li>
                <li>Right-click the source → <strong>Filters</strong> → add <strong>Chroma Key</strong>.</li>
                <img src="/images/obs/01_obs_setup_filters.png" alt="Adding a Chroma Key filter to a source in OBS" loading="lazy">
                <li>Set <em>Key Color Type</em> to <strong>Blue</strong> (or Custom → <code>#0047BB</code>) and tune <em>Similarity</em> until the blue disappears cleanly.</li>
                <img src="/images/obs/02_obs_setup_blue_chroma_key.png" alt="Chroma key settings using the blue background" loading="lazy">
            </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="step">
            <h2>Go live: Start the Virtual Camera</h2>
            <ol>
                <li>In OBS click <strong>Start Virtual Camera</strong> (bottom-right, under Controls).</li>
                <img src="/images/obs/02_start_virtal_cam.png" alt="Start Virtual Camera button in OBS" loading="lazy">
                <li>In Zoom / Google Meet / Microsoft Teams, open the camera picker and select <strong>OBS Virtual Camera</strong>.</li>
                <img src="/images/obs/03_teams_setup.png" alt="Selecting OBS Virtual Camera in Microsoft Teams" loading="lazy">
            </ol>
            <div class="tip">⚠️ On macOS, Microsoft Teams occasionally doesn't list virtual cameras due to code-signing restrictions. See the <a href="https://obsproject.com/forum/threads/obs-virtual-camera-not-showing-up-in-macos-apps.139783/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OBS forum thread</a> for the current workaround.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="step">
            <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
            <table class="trouble">
                <tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Blue box instead of transparency</td>
                    <td>Meeting isn't <strong>Public</strong> (OBS sees the login page), or you're missing <code>transparent=1</code> / the Chroma Key filter. In Path 2, raise the filter's <em>Similarity</em>.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Agenda is stale after editing</td>
                    <td>Right-click the Browser source → <em>Refresh cache of current page</em>, or toggle it off/on.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Help text or buttons visible on the overlay</td>
                    <td>Make sure the URL includes <code>chrome=0</code>.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Overlay too small / off-center</td>
                    <td>Set the Browser source to your canvas resolution (usually 1920×1080), then right-click → <em>Transform</em> → <em>Fit to screen</em>.</td>
                </tr>
            </table>
        </div>

